<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=slide-rule">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slide rule</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1622</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as slipstick</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A slide rule is a hand-operated mechanical calculator of slidable rulers, used for multiplication, division, roots, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logarithm">logarithms</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trigonometry">trigonometry</a>. It was developed in the 17th century on the logarithms of John Napier: in 1620 Edmund Gunter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> made a device with a single logarithmic scale, and in c. 1622 William Oughtred of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> combined two Gunter rules into a recognisably modern slide rule. A standard linear rule is accurate to about three significant digits. It was the most commonly used calculation tool in science and engineering until the HP-35 scientific calculator, introduced in 1972, made it largely obsolete.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slide-rule.jpg" alt="Slide rule" width="330" height="95" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roger McLassus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sliderule_2005.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1622</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Cambridge, England</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>William Oughtred · Edmund Gunter · <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Oughtred became involved in a vitriolic controversy over priority with his one-time student Richard Delamain; his own ideas were made public only in publications of his student William Forster in 1632 and 1653.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/logarithm">Logarithms<span>1614</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=slide-rule">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
